{title: The Parting Glass}
{artist: The High Kings}

{comment: Verse  1}
[C]Of [Am]all the m[F]oney that [C]e'er I [G]had,
I [C]spent it in good[C] compa[G]ny.
And a[Am]ll the ha[F]rm I've [C]ever [G]done,
ala[C]s it was to [Am]none but [F]me.

And [C]all [F]I've [C]done for [C]want [F]of [G]wit
to [F]mem'ry now I [C]can't r[G]ecall;
So [Am]fill t[F]o me the[C] parting [G]glass,
Good [C]night and joy be [Am]to you [F]all.

{comment: Chorus  1}
[So] [C]fill to [F]me the [C]parting [G]glass
And [G]drink a [Am]health whate'[F]er be[G]falls
And [Am]gently [F]rise and [F]softly [C]call
Good [C]night and joy be [G]to you [C]all

{comment: Verse  2}
[C]Of all the comrades that e'er I had,
They're sorry for my going [G]away.
And [Am]all my sweethearts that e[F]'er I [C]had,
[C]They'd wish me one more [G]day to [C]stay.
But [C]since it[Am] fell u[C]nto my [G]lot,

That [G]I should [Am]rise and [F]you should [G]not,
[Am]I gently [F]rise and softly [C]call,
[C]Good night and joy be [G]to you [C]all.

{comment: Chorus  2}
[C][So] fill to [F]me the [C]parting [G]glass
[G]And drink a [Am]health whate'e[F]r bef[G]alls
And [Am]gently [F]rise and [F]softly c[C]all
Good [C]night and joy be [G]to you [C]all

{comment: Bridge}
